Round array, unrolled cache-line walk
=====================================

Part of CACHE_FRAMEWORK: kernels kept as a separate set for cache studies
(cache locking and cache-miss analysis).

What it tests
-------------

Data-cache misses of an exactly known access sequence: the array covers 128
sets x 5 ways of 32-byte lines, one more way than a 4-way cache holds, so every
walk evicts what the previous one loaded.

What it does
------------

One partition, P1, with one periodic process.

Every 100 ms the process reads one word from each of the 640 consecutive
32-byte blocks of a 20 KB array (access_array.c: 640 unrolled loads, written in
assembly for x86_64 and AArch64).

Expected output
---------------

"Finished loop" every period.

Notes
-----

The loads can be bracketed by start/stop markers for an external memory tracer
(the byte sequences 0F A7 00 00 / 0F A6 00 00). They fault on real x86_64 CPUs
and in QEMU, so they are emitted only on x86_64 and only when
CACHE_TRACE_MARKERS is defined.

Build and run
-------------

From the top-level directory (builds every benchmark; QEMU needs only the one):

    make benchmarks                     # x86_64
    make BSP=arm64-qemu benchmarks      # arm64

    qemu-system-x86_64 -vga none -m 1G -nographic \
        -kernel benchmark/CACHE_FRAMEWORK/round_array_bis/build/x86_64-qemu/pok.elf
    qemu-system-aarch64 -M virt -cpu cortex-a53 -m 1G -nographic \
        -kernel benchmark/CACHE_FRAMEWORK/round_array_bis/build/arm64-qemu/pok.elf

To build only this benchmark, run "scons bsp=x86_64-qemu" (or bsp=arm64-qemu)
in this directory with POK_PATH set. The benchmark runs forever; quit QEMU with
Ctrl-A X.
